 ## Nothing jumps
 
-The chat thread is anchored on the **top of the newest bubble**, not on the bottom edge. Words growing inside a bubble never scroll the box, so the lines above them stay where you are reading them. The box turns a page only twice: when a new bubble starts with less than 75% of a box left under it (`Widget.HEADROOM`), and when one bubble grows taller than the box (its tail then lands mid-box, half a box at a time). If you scrolled up, nothing moves at all. The code is `Widget.Thread.anchor()` in `Widget.js` (it sets `data-turns` on the thread for the proof); the proof is `test/anchor.mjs` (fake mic, five sentences at 400): before, 27 to 38 older-line moves in the split size; after, 0 while words stream in, 5 page turns, and 0 when the reader had scrolled up. The strip has no thread to move. **Cost (2026-10-25, ask `perf`):** `anchor()` only asks for a frame; one animation frame reads layout once, however many lines were drawn or captions redrawn in it (`place_anchor()` is the old body). A replay of history refreshes the lines once after the batch (`refresh_after_batch()`), not once per line. The sheet draws only the newest `Widget.HISTORY` (50) chat lines of a long session; the rest wait behind **show earlier (N)** (`hold_history()`, `load_earlier()`: the older chunk is drawn above while the reader stays where they were; `test/earlier.mjs`). `Session.watch()` tails the file by byte range ([ext/Session/doc/watch.md](/framework/ext/Session/doc/watch.md)), and the mic's meter is written once per frame (`Dictate.paint_level()`). Measured at 4x CPU, 390 px, 207 bubbles: first bubble 8.0 to 8.8 s before, about 0.4 s after; tasks over 50 ms while talking 14 before, 0 after ([perf report](/framework/ai/sessions/2026/10/20/session-e00f55c9/sessions-and-tasks/dictation-debug/perf/report.md)). Known loss: a placement or refinement line in the shown part that points at a bubble still behind the button finds nothing to change and is not re-applied when that bubble is drawn. **Rejected:** pinning the newest line to the bottom (`overflow-anchor` plus scroll-to-bottom), because every added line shifts every older bubble up; and pinning the newest line at the very top at every turn, because it hides the reply you were reading the moment you speak.
